          "guid": "mt3mxytbgw6kdy4n",
          "message": {
            "text": "The sha is canonicalized to its full 40-char form before building the commit:<sha> mode string, so opening the same commit twice reuses one review instead of piling up duplicates.\n\nWhy\nDe-dup keys on the review's mode string (getLatestInProgressReview(repoRoot, modeStr, modeArgs)), and getModeString({type:'commit',sha}) embeds the raw sha verbatim. The Open-commit button carries whatever sha the note's SARIF provenance recorded — often abbreviated. Without canonicalization, commit:a1b2c3d and commit:a1b2c3d4e5f6… are different mode strings, so the same commit opened via a short vs full sha (or two different-length notes) would each mint a separate review. Resolving to the canonical full sha first (resolveCommitSha (0), a single git rev-parse --verify <sha>^{commit}) makes the mode string stable regardless of input spelling.\n\nBonus\nThe same rev-parse --verify doubles as existence validation — a bad/synthetic sha returns null → CommitNotFoundError → the route's 404 → the client's fail-soft toast — so a note authored in a different clone (or the demo's synthetic sha) can't create an empty, confusing review.\n\nCovered by the integration test's abbreviated-sha case (tests/integration/review-open/commit-review.test.ts): opening the full sha then an 8-char prefix reuses the same review (created:false).\n",
            "markdown": "The sha is canonicalized to its full 40-char form before building the `commit:<sha>` mode string, so opening the same commit twice reuses one review instead of piling up duplicates.\n\n### Why\nDe-dup keys on the review's `mode` string (`getLatestInProgressReview(repoRoot, modeStr, modeArgs)`), and `getModeString({type:'commit',sha})` embeds the **raw** sha verbatim. The Open-commit button carries whatever sha the note's SARIF provenance recorded — often abbreviated. Without canonicalization, `commit:a1b2c3d` and `commit:a1b2c3d4e5f6…` are different mode strings, so the same commit opened via a short vs full sha (or two different-length notes) would each mint a **separate** review. Resolving to the canonical full sha first ([`resolveCommitSha`](0), a single `git rev-parse --verify <sha>^{commit}`) makes the mode string stable regardless of input spelling.\n\n### Bonus\nThe same `rev-parse --verify` doubles as existence validation — a bad/synthetic sha returns null → `CommitNotFoundError` → the route's 404 → the client's fail-soft toast — so a note authored in a different clone (or the demo's synthetic sha) can't create an empty, confusing review.\n\nCovered by the integration test's abbreviated-sha case (`tests/integration/review-open/commit-review.test.ts`): opening the full sha then an 8-char prefix reuses the same review (`created:false`).\n"
          },
          "locations": [
            {
              "physicalLocation": {
                "artifactLocation": {
                  "uri": "src/review-open/commit-review.ts"
                },
                "region": {
                  "startLine": 34,
                  "endLine": 52,
                  "snippet": {
                    "text": "export async function openCommitReview(repoRoot: string, sha: string): Promise<OpenCommitReviewResult> {\n  // Validate + canonicalize the sha before doing any work: a bad ref would\n  // otherwise silently produce an empty diff and a confusing empty review, and\n  // canonicalizing to the full 40-char sha makes the `commit:<sha>` mode string\n  // stable so opening the same commit via different spellings reuses one review.\n  const fullSha = resolveCommitSha(repoRoot, sha);\n  if (fullSha === null) {\n    throw new CommitNotFoundError(`Commit not found: ${sha}`);\n  }\n\n  const mode: ReviewMode = { type: 'commit', sha: fullSha };\n  const modeStr = getModeString(mode);\n  const modeArgs = getModeArgs(mode);\n\n  // Reuse an in-progress review for the exact same commit (same mode string), so\n  // repeated jumps to a commit land on the same review rather than piling up\n  // duplicates. Matches `launchReview`'s in-progress reuse.\n  const existing = await getLatestInProgressReview(repoRoot, modeStr, modeArgs);\n  if (existing !== undefined) {"
                  }
                }
              }
            }
          ],
